WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.251
A detective needs two clues for two unknowns. A sector may have two unknowns, the radius r and the angle theta. Each fact about it gives one equation.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:24.850
Recall the three formulae, with theta in radians. Arc length is r theta. Area is a half r squared theta. Perimeter is two r plus r theta, two radii plus the arc.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:35.629
The trick is to remove theta. From the perimeter, r theta is P minus two r. Put that into the area, which is a half r times r theta.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:47.455
This leaves an equation in r only, usually a quadratic. It has two roots, and one may have to go. Perhaps r must be an integer, or theta must be less than two pi.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:52.364
So you get two facts about one sector, and find the radius and the angle.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:01:03.725
Step one: write each fact as an equation in r and theta. Step two: make r theta the subject of the perimeter, and put it into the area.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:13.554
Step three: solve for r, and reject any root that breaks a condition. Step four: find theta, and check both original facts.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:19.477
The trap: mixing up perimeter and arc. A perimeter includes the two radii.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:40.348
November twenty twenty one. Sector A O B has radius r, an integer, and angle theta radians. Its area is sixteen point eight square centimetres, and its perimeter is sixteen point four centimetres. Find r and theta. Eight marks.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:49.712
Here is Figure one redrawn. The sector has radius r, an angle of theta radians at O, and two facts: its area and its perimeter.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:59.410
Step one: area gives a half r squared theta equals sixteen point eight. Perimeter gives two r plus r theta equals sixteen point four.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:09.869
A tempting wrong route treats sixteen point four as the arc length. Then r is about two point oh five, not an integer, so the route fails.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:24.275
Step two: from equation two, r theta is sixteen point four minus two r. Put that into the area, and tidy. We get r squared minus eight point two r, plus sixteen point eight, equals nought.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:35.695
Step three: the formula gives r equals eight point two, plus or minus nought point two, over two. So r is four, or four point two. The integer is four.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:49.500
Step four: r theta is sixteen point four minus eight, which is eight point four. So theta is two point one. Check: a half times sixteen times two point one is sixteen point eight.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:58.143
So r is four centimetres, and theta is two point one radians. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:12.114
A common mistake is keeping both roots. Four point two also solves the quadratic, but r must be an integer. Say which root you reject, and why.

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:33.409
Now you try, from November twenty twenty five. A circle has centre O and radius fourteen centimetres. A sector A O B has perimeter forty nine centimetres. Find the area of the sector. Three marks. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:46.104
The perimeter is two r plus r theta. So twenty eight plus fourteen theta is forty nine. Theta is twenty one over fourteen, one point five radians.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:57.023
Now the area. A is a half r squared theta, a half times fourteen squared times one point five. That is one hundred and forty seven square centimetres.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:07.889
The common wrong answer treats forty nine as the arc. Then theta is three point five, and the area is three hundred and forty three. But a perimeter includes the two radii.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:15.212
Remember: write the equations, eliminate theta, solve and reject a root, then find the angle and check.
